# TaskFlow — production secrets for compose.prod.yaml
#
# Copy to .env.prod and fill in real values. NEVER commit .env.prod (see
# .gitignore — the same `.env.*` rule that protects the dev .env covers this).
#
#   docker compose --env-file .env.prod -f compose.prod.yaml build
#   docker compose --env-file .env.prod -f compose.prod.yaml --profile tools run --rm migrate
#   docker compose --env-file .env.prod -f compose.prod.yaml up -d
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE GATE — run this before every deploy:
#
#     grep -n ENTER_HERE .env.prod
#
# It must print NOTHING. Every ENTER_HERE below marks a value that has to be
# generated or obtained; leaving one in place does NOT fail loudly the way a
# blank value does, because compose's `${VAR:?}` only refuses an EMPTY string
# and `ENTER_HERE` is not empty. Some of them would then be caught at boot —
# MASTER_KEY_BASE64 and JWT_SECRET are validated as 32 bytes of base64, so the
# API dies naming them — but the Postgres role passwords would not be caught by
# anything at all: the init script and the connection URLs interpolate the SAME
# variable, so a stack whose every database role has the password `ENTER_HERE`
# starts, passes its health checks, and serves traffic. That grep is the only
# thing standing between this file and that outcome.
# ===========================================================================
#
# This file is NOT a restatement of .env.example — most of these variable
# NAMES are identical (the app reads the same Zod-validated schema in
# production as in development; see apps/api/src/config/env.ts and its
# siblings), but every VALUE here must be a real, freshly generated secret.
# Reusing a dev-secret value from .env.example in production defeats the
# entire point of this file existing.
#
# Values with no equivalent in .env.example — the Postgres role passwords, the
# MinIO root credentials, and the image tag/registry — exist only because
# compose.prod.yaml has to bootstrap the database and pick an image; see
# docker/postgres/init-prod/05-set-passwords.sh for why the role passwords are
# separate from every other secret here.
#
# Variables with a safe default in compose.prod.yaml are shown commented, at
# that default. Uncomment only what you are actually changing — an operator
# reading this file should be able to tell "left at the default" from
# "deliberately set to the same value as the default", and a commented line is
# the only way to say the first one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Images — where compose.prod.yaml pulls from, or what it tags a local build.
# Leave IMAGE_TAG at "local" to build from source on this machine; the CD
# workflow (.github/workflows/cd.yml) pushes a tag matching the git SHA.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
REGISTRY=ghcr.io/m-ishfaq
IMAGE_TAG=local

# Log verbosity for every service. `info` in production — `debug` puts request
# detail in a log stream the dozzle viewer at the bottom of this file exposes.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres — the cluster superuser, plus one password per application role.
#
# Generate each with: openssl rand -hex 24
# Hex only, deliberately — docker/postgres/init-prod/05-set-passwords.sh
# interpolates these into a single-quoted SQL literal, and hex output cannot
# contain the one character (') that would break out of it.
#
# A DIFFERENT value per role, not one password repeated. The whole point of
# sixteen roles is that a leaked credential reaches only what that role was
# granted — one shared password collapses that into a single credential with
# the union of every grant.
#
# These are consumed at FIRST BOOT (docker-entrypoint-initdb.d runs against an
# empty data directory) AND on every deploy after that, by the `ensure-roles`
# service (docker/postgres/ensure-roles.sh) — which creates only whichever of
# these roles do not exist yet on this specific cluster, idempotently, so a
# role added by a later phase gets created automatically the first time it
# deploys rather than needing a manual SSH session. Changing a value here
# later does not rotate an EXISTING role's password either way; that is an
# ALTER ROLE against the running cluster, followed by updating this file so
# the next `up` still connects.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_APP_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_AUDIT_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_REALTIME_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_COLLAB_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_NOTIFICATION_SWEEP_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_BACKLINKS_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_PLATFORM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_RECORDING_INGEST_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_SEARCH_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_AUTOMATION_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_WEBHOOK_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_API_TOKEN_AUTH_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_BILLING_SWEEP_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_INTEGRATION_AUTH_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
TASKFLOW_OPS_EVENTS_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE

# Connection pool size, per service, per role. Sixteen roles across five
# services against `max_connections=200` (compose.prod.yaml's postgres command)
# — raise this and check that arithmetic first.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Object storage — self-hosted MinIO by default (compose.prod.yaml runs it),
# or point STORAGE_ENDPOINT/these same credential vars at Cloudflare R2/S3
# instead and leave the minio container running unused. See
# compose.prod.yaml's own comment on the minio service: STORAGE_ENDPOINT must
# be reachable from the BROWSER, not just from inside the compose network —
# presigned URLs are fetched directly by the client, so an internal hostname
# here produces uploads that fail only in a real browser.
#
# MINIO_ROOT_USER / MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD are BOTH the MinIO admin credential and
# the app's STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID / STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY — compose.prod
# .yaml passes the same pair to both. Generate with `openssl rand -hex 24`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MINIO_ROOT_USER=ENTER_HERE
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE
STORAGE_ENDPOINT=ENTER_HERE

# `auto` for R2, a real region for S3, anything for MinIO.
# STORAGE_REGION=auto
# Path-style addressing. Required by MinIO (no per-bucket DNS); R2 and S3 take
# either.
# STORAGE_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
# Largest attachment accepted, in bytes. Pinned into the upload signature and
# used as the ceiling on the server-side read during virus scanning, so it
# bounds memory as well as storage. 25 MiB.
# STORAGE_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=26214400

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mail — a real SMTP relay. Resend, SES SMTP, Postmark — anything that speaks
# SMTP. Not mailpit: production email has to actually arrive, and verification
# and reset links ARE the credential for the flows that issue them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MAIL_HOST=ENTER_HERE
MAIL_PORT=587
MAIL_SECURE=true
MAIL_FROM="TaskFlow <no-reply@your-domain.example>"
# Leave both blank only if MAIL_HOST is reachable with no AUTH (rare). Every
# consumer relay (Gmail, SendGrid, Postmark, Resend, SES SMTP) requires these,
# and SmtpMailer only attempts AUTH when BOTH are present — half a pair sends
# an empty AUTH, which a relay that requires it rejects outright.
MAIL_USER=ENTER_HERE
MAIL_PASSWORD=ENTER_HERE

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cryptography — generate fresh values, never reuse .env.example's:
#   node -e "import('@taskflow/security').then(s => console.log(s.generateMasterKeyBase64()))"
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"   # JWT_SECRET
#
# Both are validated as exactly 32 bytes of base64, and MASTER_KEY_BASE64 must
# DIFFER from JWT_SECRET (enforced at boot in production —
# apps/api/src/config/env.ts's own superRefine), so one compromise cannot be
# two. Both are also read by apps/worker, which decrypts webhook signing
# secrets at delivery, and JWT_SECRET by apps/realtime and apps/collab, which
# verify the same access token the API signs.
#
# MASTER_KEY_ID is stored alongside every wrapped data key so rotation can tell
# generations apart. Rotating means adding a NEW id and keeping the old key
# loaded — a retired key that is dropped makes everything it wrapped
# permanently unreadable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MASTER_KEY_ID=mk-prod-1
MASTER_KEY_BASE64=ENTER_HERE
JWT_SECRET=ENTER_HERE

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application origin — the real public URL users load this app from.
# WEB_ORIGIN doubles as the WebSocket handshakes' allowed origin (apps/
# realtime, apps/collab) and the passkey relying-party id. Changing it after
# users have enrolled passkeys invalidates every one of them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WEB_ORIGIN=https://your-domain.example

# nginx (compose.prod.yaml's `web` service) is exactly one hop in front of
# api/realtime — 1 trusts that hop's X-Forwarded-For and nothing further out.
# See apps/api/src/config/trust-proxy.ts. Change only if a second proxy
# (a cloud load balancer, or Caddy doing ACME) sits in front of nginx too —
# then it's 2. `true` is REJECTED at boot: it makes the client address whatever
# a caller puts in the header, which turns every per-IP rate limit opt-out.
API_TRUST_PROXY=1
REALTIME_TRUST_PROXY=1

# Where the `web` container's nginx binds on the host. Defaults to every
# interface, matching the runbook's first-deploy flow (curl the bare host on
# :80 before TLS exists). Once a reverse proxy owns the public :80/:443, set
# WEB_HOST_BIND=127.0.0.1 so this container stops competing for the same port.
# WEB_HOST_BIND=0.0.0.0
# WEB_HOST_PORT=80

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — telephony (Twilio). Leave blank and every telephony route
# answers SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE rather than being registered-and-broken.
#
# Filling TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID with a real AC... SID selects the LIVE carrier and
# makes the two variables below it REQUIRED — the API refuses to boot without
# them (apps/api/src/telephony/deps.ts).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID=
# REQUIRED the moment TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID is set. 32 bytes of base64, and
# separate key material from MASTER_KEY_BASE64 on purpose — one compromise must
# not both decrypt the counterparty-number column and let an attacker generate
# indexes to confirm guesses against it:
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
TELEPHONY_INDEX_KEY=
# The public origin Twilio calls back on — usually the same as WEB_ORIGIN,
# proxied through /telephony (apps/web/nginx.conf). REQUIRED for a live
# carrier. Get it wrong and nothing fails loudly: the carrier accepts the URL,
# calls it, reaches nobody, and every status callback — which is how ACTUAL
# cost arrives — is lost, so the spend cap is charged against estimates
# indefinitely.
TELEPHONY_WEBHOOK_ORIGIN=
# The per-org spend cap in CENTS over a rolling 30 days, and the ceiling no
# self-service raise may exceed. An explicit comms.spend_policy row for an org
# always wins over the first; the second is the one bound a compromised Owner
# credential cannot move, which is why raising a cap is not the same as
# unlimited spend.
# TELEPHONY_DEFAULT_SPEND_CAP_CENTS=2500
# TELEPHONY_MAX_SPEND_CAP_CENTS=50000
# Exactly ONE instance may run the recording-ingest sweep.
# RECORDING_INGEST_ENABLED=false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — in-app voice TURN relay. Leave both blank for STUN-only, which
# connects on most networks. Set both together or not at all — the app refuses
# to boot on half a pair, because a URL with no secret hands the browser a
# relay it cannot authenticate against and ICE falls back to STUN silently.
#
# Then add `--profile turn` to `up` so the coturn container actually starts;
# without it these point at a relay that is not running.
#   openssl rand -hex 32   # RTC_TURN_SECRET
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RTC_TURN_URLS=
RTC_TURN_SECRET=
# coturn's realm, and the STUN list handed to the browser. The default STUN
# server is Google's public one — replace it if you would rather not have every
# client of this deployment resolve its public address through a third party.
# RTC_TURN_REALM=taskflow
# RTC_STUN_URLS=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302
# Credential lifetime, and the DURABLE per-org issuance budget over a rolling
# 24 hours — counted in Postgres (rtc.turn_issuance), not in process, because
# an in-memory counter forgives everyone on restart. This is the bandwidth
# analogue of the telephony spend cap: an open relay carries strangers' traffic
# on this deployment's bill.
# RTC_TURN_TTL_SECONDS=600
# RTC_TURN_ISSUANCE_CAP_PER_DAY=500
# `all` or `relay`. NOT the production setting — `relay` discards every
# non-relay candidate, so with no TURN configured no call can ever connect.
# RTC_ICE_TRANSPORT_POLICY=all
# Ceiling on one uploaded call recording, pinned into the presigned PUT's
# signature. ~64 MiB is about four hours of Opus.
# RTC_MAX_RECORDING_BYTES=67108864

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — web push (VAPID) and OAuth sign-in. Every one of these is
# independently optional: an unset VAPID pair means the preferences page
# reports push unavailable rather than pretending the channel works, and an
# unset provider pair means that sign-in button simply does not render.
#
# Leave blank rather than filling with a placeholder — for the OAuth pairs,
# "present" is what renders the button, so a placeholder produces a button that
# fails at the provider instead of one that is honestly absent.
#
#   node -e "import('@taskflow/security').then(s => console.log(JSON.stringify(s.generateVapidKeys(), null, 2)))"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VAPID_SUBJECT=
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — billing (Stripe). Leave PAYMENTS_PROVIDER unset (or `fake`) and
# every billing route runs end-to-end against the in-memory FakePaymentProvider
# — no Stripe account, no real charges, checkout/portal/webhooks all work.
#
# Setting PAYMENTS_PROVIDER=stripe switches BOTH apps/api (checkout, portal,
# webhook verification) and apps/worker (the period-close job that bills usage
# overage) to the live processor — both read the identical two variables below,
# because an api on `stripe` with a worker left on `fake` takes real money at
# checkout and records every tenant's overage against an in-memory map that
# vanishes on restart. Set every variable in this block together, or leave the
# whole block unset.
#
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY: Stripe dashboard -> Developers -> API keys (sk_live_... in
# production, sk_test_... to rehearse against a real Stripe test mode account).
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET: Developers -> Webhooks -> add endpoint at
# ${WEB_ORIGIN}/trpc/billing.webhook (or wherever apps/web/nginx.conf proxies
# it) -> the endpoint's own Signing secret (whsec_...). apps/worker does not
# need this one — it never receives webhooks, only calls the processor
# directly for period-close billing.
#
# Plans and prices are NOT env vars — they are rows in billing.plans /
# billing.plan_prices, created from the platform console (/platform-admin ->
# Plans), which writes the Product and Price to Stripe itself once this block
# is live.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PAYMENTS_PROVIDER=stripe
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_...
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# Business constants, not security boundaries — tunable with no migration.
# Both apps/api and apps/worker validate BILLING_PAST_DUE_GRACE_DAYS
# independently (their env schemas are deliberately separate), so it is set
# once here for both; BILLING_TRIAL_ENDING_WARNING_HOURS is read by
# apps/worker only, which sends the warning email.
# BILLING_TRIAL_DAYS=14
# BILLING_PAST_DUE_GRACE_DAYS=7
# BILLING_TRIAL_ENDING_WARNING_HOURS=72

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — sweeps and gateway tuning. Every value below is compose.prod
# .yaml's own default; uncomment only to change one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The chat retention sweep has no SKIP-LOCKED claim, so two instances ticking
# together both issue the same DELETE and put duplicate deletions in the
# compliance record. Exactly one instance should enable it.
# RETENTION_SWEEP_ENABLED=false

# How long before an access token expires the client proactively reconnects,
# and the outbox relay's poll interval. The poll is the CORRECTNESS floor, not
# the delivery mechanism — LISTEN/NOTIFY makes the common case fast, this
# bounds a missed notification to latency rather than a lost event.
# REALTIME_REAUTH_LEAD_SECONDS=60
# REALTIME_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=5000
# REALTIME_MAX_CONNECTIONS_PER_IP_PER_MINUTE=30
# REALTIME_MAX_JOINS_PER_MINUTE=60
# REALTIME_MAX_REFUSED_JOINS_PER_MINUTE=10
# Far above the join limit on purpose: ICE trickling legitimately emits dozens
# of candidates per peer in the first seconds of a call.
# REALTIME_MAX_SIGNALS_PER_MINUTE=600

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — the live log viewer (dozzle). Reading its stream is reading every
# container's logs, so it binds to 127.0.0.1 only and belongs behind the
# reverse proxy's own basic-auth, at DOZZLE_BASE's path. That credential is
# INFRASTRUCTURE-level and deliberately outside the app's login and role system
# — see compose.prod.yaml's own comment on the service.
#
# DOZZLE_BASE must match the proxy path it sits behind, and the Caddy side must
# use `handle`, not `handle_path`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DOZZLE_BASE=/logs
# DOZZLE_HOST_BIND=127.0.0.1
# DOZZLE_HOST_PORT=8081

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Slack/GitHub automation connectors (Phase 10 Wave 4). All optional — an
# unconfigured provider's connect route answers NOT_FOUND rather than the app
# failing to boot, and the Integrations tab hides a provider with either half
# of its credentials missing. A SEPARATE Slack app and GitHub OAuth app from
# the sign-in ones above (GOOGLE_CLIENT_*/GITHUB_CLIENT_*) — different scope,
# never shared credentials.
#
#   SLACK_CONNECTOR_CLIENT_ID / SLACK_CONNECTOR_CLIENT_SECRET
#       From your Slack app's Basic Information page.
#   SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET
#       The Slack APP's signing secret — shared by every workspace that
#       installs it, not a per-org value. Verifies inbound Slack requests.
#   GITHUB_CONNECTOR_CLIENT_ID / GITHUB_CONNECTOR_CLIENT_SECRET
#       From a GitHub OAuth App separate from the sign-in one. Its
#       Authorization callback URL must be exactly
#       ${WEB_ORIGIN}/integrations/callback/github.
#   INTEGRATION_WEBHOOK_ORIGIN
#       The absolute origin the UI builds each connector's webhook URL from.
#       Absent = webhook URLs are hidden and slice 3's inbound routes are
#       unregistered, but the connect flow itself still works. Usually the
#       same value as WEB_ORIGIN.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SLACK_CONNECTOR_CLIENT_ID=
# SLACK_CONNECTOR_CLIENT_SECRET=
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=
# GITHUB_CONNECTOR_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CONNECTOR_CLIENT_SECRET=
# INTEGRATION_WEBHOOK_ORIGIN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT WIRED INTO compose.prod.yaml — do not set this here expecting it to take
# effect. Listed so the gap is visible rather than discovered by a feature
# silently not working in production.
#
#   AUTOMATION_TELEPHONY_ACTIONS_ENABLED
#       The cost-bearing automation actions stay off in production regardless
#       of what is set here. That one is off in the SAFE direction.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
